l engine with a super‑efficient one. We’re comparing: - **Gas furnace:** turns 1 unit of gas into ~0.95 units of heat (cheap fuel, slightly imperfect efficiency). - **Electric resistance heater:** turns 1 unit of expensive electricity into ~1 unit of heat (expensive fuel, perfect efficiency). There’s no massive efficiency advantage to cancel out the higher price of the fuel. So the simple electric heater costs about 3–4 times more to run. --- ### 3. The “cleaner” part is true, and that’s exactly why policy is pushing electricity anyway You’re absolutely right that electricity can be much cleaner than burning fossil fuels at home, especially as grids decarbonize. That’s why many governments are encouraging a switch to **electric heat pumps**—not because electricity per unit is always cheaper, but because: - A heat pump can deliver **3–5 units of heat for every 1 unit of electricity**, making the effective cost competitive with (or cheaper than) gas even at today’s retail prices. - It lets us heat homes with wind and solar electricity instead of gas, cutting carbon emissions even if the raw cost is similar. So the underlying idea—moving towards clean electricity for everything—is correct. But we need to use the *right* electric devices to make it affordable. For cars, a direct motor swap already gives a huge efficiency gain and saves money. For home heat, a direct swap to a plain electric furnace gives no such gain; you need the heat pump’s “magic” to multiply the energy before it becomes the cheaper option. --- **In short:** The statement “electricity is cheaper” usually refers to the cost of *generating* a megawatt‑hour at a solar farm, not the price you pay at your thermostat. The retail price of electricity is still higher than that of natural gas per unit of energy in most regions. Electric cars win because they *use* far fewer units; electric heat needs a heat pump to win by the same logic.
