23:30:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really important piece of evidence, and I’m glad you brought it up. Your experience is absolutely valid—many people have lowered their bills by switching from a whole-house gas furnace to using electric space heaters. But what’s probably happening in your case actually highlights some key nuances rather than overturning the general rule. Let’s explore why your experience makes perfect sense **without** meaning that electricity is a cheaper fuel per unit of heat. It usually comes down to **how** you’re using that heat, not just the fuel type. --- ### 1. The zoning effect: heating only the rooms you occupy A central gas furnace typically pushes warm air through ducts to heat the entire house to the thermostat setting, including empty bedrooms, hallways, and unused spaces—often 24/7 if you don’t have a programmable setback. When you switch to portable electric heaters (space heaters), most people naturally start **zone heating**—they only heat the room they’re in and keep the rest of the house much cooler. Even though electric resistance heat costs more *per unit of heat*, the total number of units you’re buying can drop so dramatically that the bill goes down. You might have unintentionally performed a classic energy-saving experiment: paying a higher price for a much smaller amount of heat delivered exactly where and when you need it. That often beats buying a larger amount of cheaper heat spread all over the house. --- ### 2. Duct losses and old furnace inefficiencies A natural gas furnace might have a high efficiency rating (AFUE of 80–97%) in the lab, but real-world performance can be degraded by: - **Duct leaks** (typical ducts lose 20–30% of heated air into attics or crawl spaces). - **Poor insulation** around ductwork. - **Old, poorly maintained burners** that don’t hit their rated efficiency. Electric resistance heaters, on the other hand, deliver 100% of their electricity as heat right into the living space, with no distribution losses. If your gas system was leaking a lot of heat outside the living area, your effective cost per useful therm of heat into the rooms might have been much higher than the raw gas price would suggest. --- ### 3. Local energy prices might be unusual for your area While in most of the US gas is $0.03–$0.05/kWh and electricity $0.10–$0.25/kWh, there are places where this relationship is reversed or much narrower: - **Cheap hydro power** (e.g., Pacific Northwest, Quebec) can give residential rates below $0.08/kWh. - **High gas prices** (e.g., parts of the Northeast during supply crunches, or many European countries recently) can push gas to $0.08–$0.12/kWh of thermal energy. - **Electricity price plans** might also offer time-of-use rates that make running heaters at night much cheaper. In such cases, even whole-house electric resistance heat can compete with or beat gas. But those regions are the exception, not the rule, which is why “all houses” haven’t switched. --- ### 4. Different winter severity from one year to the next It’s also possible that the winter you switched simply had milder weather, milder than the one before. A warmer winter can easily cut heating bills by 20–40%, making any new device look amazing. People often compare bills month-to-month or year-to-year and naturally attribute the change to the new heaters, when in fact a big part of it was less heating demand. (Utilities sometimes provide “degree day” data so you can adjust for weather.) --- ### Putting it together: your experience is a smart heating strategy, not a fuel cost victory What your experience really shows is that **how you heat** can trump the cost of the fuel itself. Using cheap gas inefficiently (heating empty rooms, losing heat through ducts) can cost more than using expensive electricity very sparingly with pinpoint accuracy. But if you had installed an **electric furnace** or **whole-house electric baseboards** that heated the home just like your gas system used to (every room to the same temperature, same schedule), your bill would likely have jumped way up. In fact, most people who have lived with whole-house electric resistance heat in a cold climate can attest to the shocking bills. --- ### So why doesn’t everyone do what you did? Mainly because: - Many people value whole-house comfort and don’t want to live in a 60°F house except for one warm room. - Space heaters can be a fire hazard if not used properly and don’t work well for plumbing freeze protection in extreme cold. - The gains you got from zoning can also be achieved with a gas system using zoned dampers or smart vents—but that’s a more complex retrofit. The broader trend isn’t to adopt space heaters as the primary system, but to install **heat pumps** (which can zap the cost advantage of gas even for whole-house heating) combined with smart zoning. That way you get the comfort and the low cost. Thanks again for sharing that—it’s a perfect real-world illustration that **energy bills are about system design and behavior, not just fuel type**. And it explains why moving to all-electric heat isn’t automatic: simply swapping a furnace for electric resistance at the whole-house level generally costs more, unless we either use the heater differently (like you did) or deploy more efficient electric tech (heat pumps).
